  "framing": "G6 scope clarification (resolves C<n(Q)*log K paradox): there are TWO distinct communication models. (1) STRUCTURE-TRANSMITTED model: the covering set (which nodes are aggregated) must itself be communicated, cost = covering-set entropy log2(C(N,m)) ~ m*log(N/m)+m; Theorem-1's geometric term n(Q)*log K is a valid lower bound for ORDERED node-ID transmission here (verified exhaustively in G7, and n(Q)*log K is a loose UPPER reference that exceeds the true entropy by up to 2.27x). (2) STRUCTURE-IMPLIED model (the construction): the query geometry Q is PUBLIC/known, so the covering structure is implied by Q and need not be transmitted; communication is only the n(Q) count VALUES, at ~1.2 bits each = C=1.2*n(Q) bits. Hence C<covering-set entropy (0/27 configs) is CORRECT and expected: the construction does NOT pay the structure-identification cost. Therefore C<n(Q)*log K for log K>1.2 does NOT contradict Theorem 1 -- the two bounds are for different models (structure-implied vs structure-transmitted), and the construction is near-optimal for the query-geometry-public setting that the paper's federated range-query protocol actually operates in."
